  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nThough ridging aloft will begin to weaken during the back half of \nthe week, low-lvl thermal profiles will lag behind a bit as 850mb \ncontinue to rise toward 26-27C into Thursday. This will result in \nsfc highs reaching 102-108 degrees across the western deserts, again \nnearing daily records in Yuma and the Imperial Valley. Major \nHeatRisk is expected to be most expansive on Thursday afternoon, \nleading to a spatial extension of the Extreme Heat Warning to \ninclude the entire Lower Colorado River Valley and a portion of the \nGila River Valley in Yuma County including Dateland and Tacna.\nPortions of south-central AZ will also see highs climb to around \n100-103 or around 10 degrees above normal on Thursday, although the \nHeatRisk is expected to remain in the Moderate category. Friday will \nbe a transition day where a deepening trough over the Pac NW which \nin turn induces SW flow aloft over the West Coast. Although we will \nbegin to see a reduction in overall hghts aloft on Friday afternoon, \nlow-lvl thermal profiles will be slow to respond as 850 mb \ntemperatures are still expected to reach 25-26C across the much of \nthe forecast area equating to sfc highs around 100-105 degrees in \nmost lower desert communities. Due to these temperatures and \nlingering Major HeatRisk for the Imperial Valley and Yuma area, the \nExtreme Heat Warning will continue through Friday.\n\nWe then turn our attention to an upcoming pattern shift heading into \nthis weekend as most deterministic models and ensemble members are \ntrending toward an overall troffing pattern setting up across the \nwestern CONUS. There is now better model agreement that a stronger \nand deeper trough will transition into a cutoff low over the Pac NW \non Saturday and gradually sink southward into CA Sunday and Monday. \nMeanwhile, latest forecast guidance from the NHC shows an eventual \nturn to the northeast by Rachel this weekend with the remnants of \nthe storm itself being pulled northeastward and phasing with the \nwest coast trough. As this pattern transpires, there should be an \nincrease in southwesterly flow aloft over the Desert Southwest which \nwill begin to pull Pacific moisture into the region by late Saturday \ninto Sunday. Coincidentally, rain chances will return to the much of \nthe region with NBM PoPs now around 20-50% across south-central AZ \non Sunday and even up to 40-60% across the foothills NE of Phoenix \nby Monday. There is still high uncertainty in rainfall amounts at \nthis time but there is at least a chance of some higher totals \nexceeding 1.50\" across AZ high terrain. As 500 mb hghts begin to \nlower ahead of the approaching trough along with increasing cloud \ncover and moisture, high temperatures will take a dive from the mid \n90s on Saturday to the low to mid 80s by Monday.",
